examples.question_text:
A strip has $\frac{5}{8}$ shaded.
[tikz]% mathsmap-diagram-colours {"version":1,"kind":"geometry","base":[],"semantic":[{"name":"templateShade","hex":"D4E8FF","reason":"Shows the given five shaded eighths of one whole strip."}],"reference":"template strip-given"}
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=1mm,y=1mm]
\special{dvisvgm:raw <metadata data-graph-strokes="1"/>}
\definecolor{templateShade}{HTML}{D4E8FF}
\path[use as bounding box] (-0.6,-12.6) rectangle (64.6,0.6);
\fill[templateShade] (0,-12) rectangle (8,0);
\fill[templateShade] (8,-12) rectangle (16,0);
\fill[templateShade] (16,-12) rectangle (24,0);
\fill[templateShade] (24,-12) rectangle (32,0);
\fill[templateShade] (32,-12) rectangle (40,0);
\draw[black,line width=0.4pt] (8,0)--(8,-12);
\draw[black,line width=0.4pt] (16,0)--(16,-12);
\draw[black,line width=0.4pt] (24,0)--(24,-12);
\draw[black,line width=0.4pt] (32,0)--(32,-12);
\draw[black,line width=0.4pt] (40,0)--(40,-12);
\draw[black,line width=0.4pt] (48,0)--(48,-12);
\draw[black,line width=0.4pt] (56,0)--(56,-12);
\draw[black,line width=0.8pt] (0,0) rectangle (64,-12);
\end{tikzpicture}[/tikz]
(a) What fraction is unshaded? Check that the two parts make one whole.
(b) Why must one whole be renamed as eighths before subtracting?

examples.solution_text:
(a) One whole is $\frac{8}{8}$.
$\begin{aligned}&\mathrel{\phantom{=}}1-\frac{5}{8}\\&=\frac{8}{8}-\frac{5}{8}\\&=\frac{3}{8}\end{aligned}$
The unshaded part is $\frac{3}{8}$. The diagram below highlights the three remaining eighths.
[tikz]% mathsmap-diagram-colours {"version":1,"kind":"geometry","base":[],"semantic":[{"name":"templateShade","hex":"D4E8FF","reason":"Highlights the three eighths that were unshaded in the question diagram; this is a solution annotation."}],"reference":"template strip-remaining"}
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=1mm,y=1mm]
\special{dvisvgm:raw <metadata data-graph-strokes="1"/>}
\definecolor{templateShade}{HTML}{D4E8FF}
\path[use as bounding box] (-0.6,-12.6) rectangle (64.6,0.6);
\fill[templateShade] (40,-12) rectangle (48,0);
\fill[templateShade] (48,-12) rectangle (56,0);
\fill[templateShade] (56,-12) rectangle (64,0);
\draw[black,line width=0.4pt] (8,0)--(8,-12);
\draw[black,line width=0.4pt] (16,0)--(16,-12);
\draw[black,line width=0.4pt] (24,0)--(24,-12);
\draw[black,line width=0.4pt] (32,0)--(32,-12);
\draw[black,line width=0.4pt] (40,0)--(40,-12);
\draw[black,line width=0.4pt] (48,0)--(48,-12);
\draw[black,line width=0.4pt] (56,0)--(56,-12);
\draw[black,line width=0.8pt] (0,0) rectangle (64,-12);
\end{tikzpicture}[/tikz]
Check: $\frac{5}{8}+\frac{3}{8}=\frac{8}{8}=1$.
(b) The parts must be the same size. Renaming $1$ as $\frac{8}{8}$ lets us subtract the number of eighths while keeping the denominator $8$.
